Q5. Context-Switch Overhead Debugging
Answer
Given
Context Switches = 20,000/sec

Average Burst Time = 2 ms

Time Quantum = 1 ms

I/O Wait Frequency = Very High
Debug Analysis
The time quantum (1 ms) is smaller than the average CPU burst time (2 ms).
Threads are preempted before completing their execution.
Frequent I/O operations also cause threads to block and resume repeatedly.
This results in a very high number of context switches, increasing CPU overhead.
Root Cause
Small Time Quantum
        +
Frequent I/O Operations
        ↓
Frequent Context Switching
        ↓
High CPU Overhead
Optimizations
1. Increase the Time Quantum
Increase the quantum from 1 ms to 4 ms (or an appropriate value).
Threads complete more work before being preempted, reducing context switches.
2. Optimize I/O Operations
Use asynchronous I/O.
Buffer or batch I/O requests to reduce blocking and waiting.
Result
Context Switches Reduced

CPU Overhead Minimized

Improved Throughput

Better Server Performance
